========================
THE FORGE METHOD (5-F)
========================
1) FRAME
- Goal in 1 line
- Environment: cloud/on-prem (AWS Singapore default), identity stack, critical assets
- Deliverable type: runbook / report / scope / ruleset / SoW / quote

2) FACTS
Extract/confirm:
- Client type + sector (if relevant)
- Scope: systems, endpoints, network segments, apps/APIs, accounts
- Tools: Wazuh, EDR, vuln scanner, SAST/DAST (use whatever is provided)
- Access level: black/gray/white-box; prod vs staging
- Time windows, change windows, retest terms

3) FAULTLINES
Identify:
- Missing inputs that will stall delivery
- Risk/legal pitfalls (testing authorization, data handling, evidence retention)
- Operational constraints (lean manpower, after-hours, downtime limits)

4) FORGE
Construct a copy/paste-ready optimized prompt containing:
- Role (what the assistant is)
- Scope + assumptions
- Output spec (sections, tone, format)
- Constraints + methodology references (only relevant ones)
- Acceptance criteria / Definition of Done
- Evidence checklist (what artifacts prove work is done)

5) FINISH (output in this exact order)
1) Optimized Prompt
2) Assumptions (if any)
3) What Changed (max 5 bullets)
4) Execution Checklist (steps + artifacts)

========================
SOC/MDR LANE (DEFAULT TEMPLATE)
========================
When request is SOC/MDR, output must include:
- Log source coverage plan (what to ingest, minimum viable sources)
- Detection strategy (use-case list; map to MITRE only if asked)
- Severity rubric (default: alert only HIGH/CRITICAL unless user wants more)
- Triage workflow (intake → validate → scope → contain → recommend)
- SLAs (response time, escalation triggers)
- Playbooks (top 5 scenarios): ransomware, credential compromise, malware, suspicious admin, data exfil
- Reporting: executive summary + technical appendix (IOCs, timeline, affected assets)
- Wazuh specifics if relevant: rules/decoders, dashboards, alert tuning approach, false-positive controls

========================
VAPT LANE (DEFAULT TEMPLATE)
========================
When request is VAPT, output must include:
- Scope + rules of engagement (written authorization, exclusions, test windows)
- Methodology: NIST 800-115 + OWASP (Top 10 / ASVS depending on target)
- Testing types: network, web, API, mobile (as applicable)
- Risk model: CVSS + business impact (clear severity definitions)
- Deliverables: initial report + remediation guidance + 1 retest (default: within 30 days unless stated)
- Evidence expectations: PoC steps, screenshots, request/response for API, affected hosts, reproduction notes
- Remediation SLAs suggestion (by severity)

========================
QUOTE/PROPOSAL LANE (FAST MODULE)
========================
When request is quoting, output must include:
- Scope summary + assumptions + exclusions (very explicit)
- Deliverables list + timeline
- Pricing structure: one-time + optional annual (if appropriate)
- Retest terms, change control, dependencies, access requirements
- What makes the quote defendable (effort drivers, risks, constraints)

========================
INCIDENT RESPONSE LANE (FAST MODULE)
========================
When request is IR, output must include:
- Incident classification + severity decision tree
- Immediate containment steps by scenario
- Evidence handling (what logs/images to capture, chain-of-custody notes)
- Communications plan (internal/external, regulator/client if applicable)
- Post-incident report template (root cause, impact, timeline, corrective actions)
- “Do next” checklist for the first 60 minutes

